exchange rate volatilities in Croatia"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"In accordance to Lang (2005), exchange rate vola tility is the main driving force of Croatian foreign exchange intervention while the exchange rate level turns out to be insignificant.  According to Chmelarova and Schnabl (2006) Croatian foreign exchange intervention manages   both   day-to-day exchange rate volatilities as well as exchange rate levels. Furthermore, the authors concluded that the pattern of foreign exchange intervention for Croa tia confirms a fear of depreciation (with respect to balance sheet effects of the banking sector) more than a fear of appreciation (with respect to export competitiveness) . Following the previous research results and empirical data on exchange rate movements, the EURIHRK   exchange rate might be strictly controlled by the Croatian National Bank. Since the Croatian National Bank cannot control the USDIHRK exchange rate at the same time; its interesting to compare the EURIHRK and the USDI HRK volatilities pattern.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":401.64833577473956,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":40.333333333333336,"r":237.0,"t":618.9816691080729},"charspan":[0,968],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/1","text":"In accordance to Lang (2005), exchange rate vola tility is the main driving force of Croatian foreign exchange intervention while the exchange rate level turns out to be insignificant.  According to Chmelarova and Schnabl (2006) Croatian foreign exchange intervention manages   both   day-to-day exchange rate volatilities as well as exchange rate levels. Furthermore, the authors concluded that the pattern of foreign exchange intervention for Croa tia confirms a fear of depreciation (with respect to balance sheet effects of the banking sector) more than a fear of appreciation (with respect to export competitiveness) . Following the previous research results and empirical data on exchange rate movements, the EURIHRK   exchange rate might be strictly controlled by the Croatian National Bank. Since the Croatian National Bank cannot control the USDIHRK exchange rate at the same time; its interesting to compare the EURIHRK and the USDI HRK volatilities pattern."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"4 Research data","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":356.98166910807294,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":42.333333333333336,"r":112.0,"t":367.64833577473956},"charspan":[0,15],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/2","text":"4 Research data"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Financial time series often exhibit volatility cluster meaning that high volatility periods tend to be followed by high volatility periods and low volatility periods tend to be followed by low volatility periods. In that case, a strong autocorrelation in squared returns or autoregressive conditional  heteroscedas ticity is present As a consequence, the least squares estimators are still unbiased but inefficient. The es timates of the variances are biased, thus invalidating the tests of significance; and the obtained results are dubious (see for example Erjavec and Cota, 2007). In order to resolve the problem and obtain estima tor efficiency; method of estimation several ARCH type models has been employed. As in most empirical finance literature, the variable to be eled is the daily exchange rate return which is the first difference of the natural logarithm of the exchange rate and is given by the following equation: ing mod -","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":145.31500244140625,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":40.333333333333336,"r":237.33333333333334,"t":342.98166910807294},"charspan":[0,939],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/3","text":"Financial time series often exhibit volatility cluster meaning that high volatility periods tend to be followed by high volatility periods and low volatility periods tend to be followed by low volatility periods. In that case, a strong autocorrelation in squared returns or autoregressive conditional  heteroscedas ticity is present As a consequence, the least squares estimators are still unbiased but inefficient. The es timates of the variances are biased, thus invalidating the tests of significance; and the obtained results are dubious (see for example Erjavec and Cota, 2007). In order to resolve the problem and obtain estima tor efficiency; method of estimation several ARCH type models has been employed. As in most empirical finance literature, the variable to be eled is the daily exchange rate return which is the first difference of the natural logarithm of the exchange rate and is given by the following equation: ing mod -"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"S ,","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":123.64833577473962,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":83.33333333333333,"r":94.0,"t":135.64833577473962},"charspan":[0,3],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Where r is the daily exchange rate return and S, and S,-1 denote the Croatian National Bank (CNB) mid point exchange rate of the EUR versus the HRK and the USD versus the HRK at the current and pre day vious respectively. The data span from Ist Janu ary 1997 to 3Ot September 2015 is used as data sample for modeling the daily exchange rate return. Table 1 shows the descriptive statistics the ob served variables. day for","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":52.68681182709872,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":40.333333333333336,"r":236.33333333333334,"t":99.31500244140625},"charspan":[0,201],"page_no":1},{"bbox":{"b":564.9816691080729,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":245.0,"r":441.0,"t":619.2637010382607},"charspan":[202,422],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"Where r is the daily exchange rate return and S, and S,-1 denote the Croatian National Bank (CNB) mid point exchange rate of the EUR versus the HRK and the USD versus the HRK at the current and pre day vious respectively. The data span from Ist Janu ary 1997 to 3Ot September 2015 is used as data sample for modeling the daily exchange rate return. Table 1 shows the descriptive statistics the ob served variables. day for"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Table 1 Descriptive statistics for the daily exchange rates return of the EUR and the USD versus the HRK","parent":{"$ref":"#/tables/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":516.9816691080729,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":245.66666666666666,"r":427.3333333333333,"t":548.3150024414062},"charspan":[0,104],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"Table 1 Descriptive statistics for the daily exchange rates return of the EUR and the USD versus the HRK"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Source: Authors' calculation","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":331.64833577473956,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":246.0,"r":352.0,"t":340.98166910807294},"charspan":[0,28],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/7","text":"Source: Authors' calculation"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"A feature of exchange rate returns is that the distribution of returns is fat tailed. That is, the ability density function of exchange rate returns appears to be leptokurtic, s0 it is more peaked at the center and has fatter tails compared to that of the normal distribution.  Numerically the kurtosis CO efficient is found to be greater than 3, which char acterizes kurtosis of a normally distributed random be slightly skewed either to the left or to the right which is not consistent with returns being normally distributed. Ihe positive value of skewness indicates that data are skewed to the right referring to a depreciation of the currency (HRK). As can be seen in Table 1, the exchange rate returns series (r) exhibits significant values of skewness and kurtosis, and therefore normality assumptions are not met. In accordance to abovementioned alternative, dis tributions have to be used as basis for modeling exchange rate returns, such as the Student-t or Gen eralized Error distribution (GED) rather than the normal distribution which takes into account the phenomenon of leptokurtosis and skewness in the probability density function. key prob -","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":54.64833577473962,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":245.0,"r":442.0,"t":316.64833577473956},"charspan":[0,1159],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/8","text":"A feature of exchange rate returns is that the distribution of returns is fat tailed. That is, the ability density function of exchange rate returns appears to be leptokurtic, s0 it is more peaked at the center and has fatter tails compared to that of the normal distribution.  Numerically the kurtosis CO efficient is found to be greater than 3, which char acterizes kurtosis of a normally distributed random be slightly skewed either to the left or to the right which is not consistent with returns being normally distributed. Ihe positive value of skewness indicates that data are skewed to the right referring to a depreciation of the currency (HRK). As can be seen in Table 1, the exchange rate returns series (r) exhibits significant values of skewness and kurtosis, and therefore normality assumptions are not met. In accordance to abovementioned alternative, dis tributions have to be used as basis for modeling exchange rate returns, such as the Student-t or Gen eralized Error distribution (GED) rather than the normal distribution which takes into account the phenomenon of leptokurtosis and skewness in the probability density function. key prob -"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"84 EKONOMSKI VJESNIK ECONVIEWS God. XXIX, BR. 1/2016. str: 81-94","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":36.31500244140625,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":53.333333333333336,"r":311.